# Python bytecode. The operator layer's test suite generates this the first
# time it runs, and step 9 runs it INSIDE the new vault. Without this line the
# vault's very first commit contains a machine-specific compiled artifact
# pinned to whatever Python version happened to be installed.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# macOS and Windows directory metadata. Never wanted, always generated.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# Obsidian per-machine UI state: which panes are open, sidebar widths, the
# active tab. Obsidian rewrites this every time it quits, so tracking it means
# a diff on every session that touched the vault, and a merge conflict on every
# machine that syncs it.
#
# The scaffold still WRITES this file to disk, so the owner's first launch
# opens on Home rather than an empty pane. It is simply not tracked after
# that. The tradeoff: cloning this vault to a second machine gives a default
# layout instead of the seeded one, which is the right trade against a
# permanently dirty working tree.
.obsidian/workspace.json
.obsidian/workspace-mobile.json

# Obsidian's own trash. Deleted notes land here when "Move to Obsidian trash"
# is the configured delete behaviour. Recoverable in the app, not worth
# versioning.
.trash/

# Secrets. Nothing in a scaffolded vault should need these, which is exactly
# why the line belongs here before something does.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Not ignored, deliberately:
#   .backups/   snapshots of canonical files taken before in-place edits.
#               These are the recovery path when git history is not enough,
#               so they are tracked on purpose. Note that git cannot track an
#               empty directory: the scaffold drops a .gitkeep in here (and in
#               Resources/attachments/ and _inbox/_processed/) so the folder
#               actually survives a clone. Without it, "tracked on purpose"
#               above is a claim the repository does not honour.
#   .obsidian/  everything else under it is real vault configuration and
#               belongs in version control.
